\f1\fs22 Alexander made a mental note to fix that after the succession dust had settled.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Eloise alighted first, wearing her shoes. She led them along a broad passage past dark offices. Around a corner, a corridor became dimmer and carried palpable are-you-meant-to-be-here vibes.\
Sarah was struggling to keep up, and she hated it\'97not a sick day in her life, reduced to motherhood. She\'92d found her nemesis. At least her sense of direction was holding up, or she thought it was. They\'92d been generally heading away from the labs, the Ironing Room, and even the Laundry. With Kathy at her elbow, she followed the others through a door on the left and down a long, ramped passage that led to a circular hinged hatch you could drive a compact car through. The hatch had a slotted window in it that reported nothing of the interior without pressing your nose against it. To Sarah, it felt as though they might be about to board something nautical\'85 or astro-nautical.\
Eloise entered a code on an integrated panel of glyphs on the hatch, reconsidered, and entered another.\
They passed through a short tunnel and emerged into a living room. Not large by Eloise standards, but the space and appointments suggested mid-sized mansion, professionally decorated.\
Chip expected bunks, possibly benches bolted to concrete, with a drain in the floor for occasions when accommodation became interrogation. Instead there were sofas, low tables,  lamps, a possibly functioning fireplace, a TV panel on a wall that could service a small cinema. There was a bar and shelves of showy bottles. One wall was almost entirely shelves that allowed glimpses to more room beyond, and displayed books, small sculpted pieces, glassware and bric-a-bric. Next to the fireplace, a corridor you could push a dining table sideways down led to spaces unknown.\
\'93Bedrooms and bathrooms,\'94 Eloise said, indicating the corridor.\
Sarah congratulated herself for lowering her belly cargo semi-gracefully into a sofa while Kathy collected cushions, most of which Sarah immediately rejected. One had a large embroidered bird whose beak seemed positioned to probe rather than comfort.\
Eloise removed her shoes. \'93There\'92s food,\'94 she said and retreated beyond the shelving.\
\'93Where?\'94 Chip queried. He opened cupboards and found only things you find in forgotten living room cupboards\'97not quite vintage furniture polish, incomplete card decks, an empty guest book, a biscuit tin full of curtain hooks, old batteries, a chocolate box full of dead ants, price tags attached to napkin rings, or a blackened silver cake slice\'97those are just some of the things Eloise would never be caught stuffing in a living room cupboard. Those bunker cupboards were bare, so there.\
He went to the bar, found a fridge and an espresso machine with two grinders.\
Alexander explored the corridor. He opened the first door, inspected and moved on. The second detained him longer.\
\'93This one has a desk,\'94 he announced.\
\'93Take it,\'94 Sarah said.\
\'93And a decent chair. I could work in here.\'94\
\'93Perfect.\'94\
Eloise returned and sought Alexander out. \'93Do you like this one?\'94 she asked.\
Alexander emerged from the ensuite, sniffing a bottle of cologne. \'93Sure.\'94\
Eloise backed out of the room and touched the panel beside the door. A small indicator changed colour.\
Inside, Alexander tried the handle.\
\'93Privacy,\'94 Eloise called through the door.\
He tried it again.\
\'93Yours or mine?\'94\
Eloise returned to the living room.\
Kathy had persuaded Sarah to put her feet up. Chip was attempting to take his shoes off without bending enough to make kindling of the chair he was sitting on.\
Hayley remained standing. \'93Where can I see what\'92s happening?\'94\
Eloise smiled; she appreciated the question. \'93Come with me.\'94\

